as the first patients were released from  temporary hospitals in Wuhan City on  Tuesday there was a glimmer of hope that  China's efforts to contain the corona  virus may be succeeding reassuring  perhaps for the 50 million people on  lockdown under quarantine measures meant  to stop the spread of the disease now  known as kovat 19 the World Health  Organization has praised China's  response but it's worried it could  spread to poor countries if this virus  makes it to a weaker health system it  will create havoc it depends on how  really we lead this response and how how  we respond to the outbreak the  scientific community still doesn't know  a lot about the virus and a vaccine  probably won't be ready for another 18  months but China's leading  epidemiologist says the outbreak will  soon plateau at least in China margin  though the rate of new infections is  gradually declining according to the  analysis of our mathematical model the  epidemic will reach its peak in the  south regions after the middle of  February or in the second half of the  month but no one really knows for sure  and thousands of Kovan 19 patients are  in critical condition in the epicenter  of Wuhan experts say the incubation  period could be longer than first  thought up to 24 days before patients  show any symptoms Chinese officials have  launched a mass disinfection campaign in  cities far away from the epidemic to  keep it at bay and in Wuhan itself  patients are doing what they can to pass  the time before they too can walk out of  quarantine Andrew Chappell al-jazeera
 

